---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import CaseStudyLayout from "~/layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseSection from "~/components/CaseSection.astro";
import { project } from "~/lib/projects";

const p = project("gatehouse");

const verdicts = [
  ["GO", "every configured requirement is satisfied by observed evidence", "go"],
  ["REVIEW", "mergeable, but something deserves human judgment", "neutral"],
  ["BLOCKED", "a hard requirement is failing", "blocked"],
  ["DRAFT", "the author says it is not ready", "neutral"],
  ["UNKNOWN", "evidence is missing — Gatehouse does not guess", "neutral"],
] as const;
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  slug="gatehouse"
  description="Case study: Gatehouse, a local read-only GitHub PR readiness dashboard in C# and .NET 10. Branch state, checks, reviews and policy become one deterministic verdict — and missing evidence stays UNKNOWN."
>
  <div class="container-page">
    <figure class="reveal">
      <div class="media-frame shadow-lift">
        <Image
          src={p.images.dashboard!}
          alt="The Gatehouse dashboard listing pull requests with deterministic readiness verdicts and the evidence behind each one"
          widths={[640, 1080, 1600, 2000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 1152px, 94vw"
          loading="eager"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="case-caption">
        One dashboard, one deterministic verdict per pull request
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <CaseSection
    number="01"
    label="The problem"
    title="“Can this merge?” lives in five different tabs."
  >
    <p>
      Answering it means checking branch state, CI checks, review approvals, unresolved threads,
      linked issues, and repository policy — each in its own corner of GitHub. Gatehouse collects
      those facts locally and reduces them, with documented rules, to a single answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same rules drive the web UI, the CLI, reports, and JSON output — so the dashboard, the
      terminal, and your scripts can never disagree about what ready means.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <!-- verdicts -->
  <section class="container-page pt-16 md:pt-24">
    <div class="reveal hairline-t pt-4">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <span class="text-accent">02</span><span class="mx-1.5" aria-hidden="true">—</span>Five
        verdicts, zero vibes
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10 max-w-3xl">
      {
        verdicts.map(([verdict, meaning, kind], i) => (
          <div
            class="reveal hairline-t flex flex-col gap-2 py-5 sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:gap-8"
            style={`--reveal-i:${i}`}
          >
            <span
              class:list={[
                "verdict-chip",
                kind === "go" && "verdict-chip--go",
                kind === "blocked" && "verdict-chip--blocked",
              ]}
            >
              {verdict}
            </span>
            <span class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{meaning}</span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="case-prose mt-8 max-w-[46rem]">
      <strong>UNKNOWN is the load-bearing verdict.</strong> When the GitHub API cannot supply a fact —
      a permission is missing, a check has not reported — Gatehouse says so instead of optimistically
      rounding up. A decision aid that guesses is worse than none.
    </p>
  </section>

  <CaseSection number="03" label="Policy as configuration" width="wide">
    <div class="grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="case-prose">
        <p>
          Defaults are safe, and a repository can declare its own rules in a
          <code>.gatehouse.yml</code> next to where you run the CLI. Require linked issues or don’t; block
          on requested changes or treat them as advisory; demand a current branch or accept a stale one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gatehouse stays a <strong>decision aid</strong> — GitHub branch protection remains the authority,
          and Gatehouse never merges, comments, pushes, or runs Git. Its GitHub access is read-only, and
          the local SQLite cache keeps your data on your machine.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="media-frame shadow-soft">
          <div class="media-inner overflow-hidden bg-sunken">
            <div class="window-chrome"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <pre
              class="overflow-x-auto px-6 py-6 font-mono text-[0.78rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted"><code>readiness:
  require_linked_issue: false
  require_all_checks: true
  require_approval: true
  require_no_unresolved_threads: true
  require_mergeable: true
  require_current_branch: false
  block_on_changes_requested: true</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="case-caption">.gatehouse.yml — the whole policy surface</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="04" label="Engineering decisions" width="wide">
    <dl class="grid gap-x-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="decision reveal">
        <dt>One rules engine, four surfaces</dt>
        <dd>
          The readiness model is a single deterministic core consumed by the Blazor dashboard, the
          CLI, report output, and JSON. Testing the rules once tests every surface.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="decision reveal">
        <dt>Demo mode with synthetic data</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>gatehouse status --demo</code> exercises the full product with synthetic data and makes
          no GitHub or network request at all — you can evaluate it before trusting it with a token.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="decision reveal">
        <dt>Shipped as a dotnet tool</dt>
        <dd>
          Distribution is a NuGet package installed with <code>dotnet tool install</code>. The
          release gate installs and runs the packed tool on hosted macOS and Windows, while Linux
          runs the full build, tests, browser flow, and server smoke test.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="decision reveal">
        <dt>Tokens stay out of the repository</dt>
        <dd>
          Private repositories need a read-only token supplied by environment variable. The docs are
          explicit: never put a token in a command, config file, or repository.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="05" label="Stack and links">
    <p>
      C# · .NET 10 · ASP.NET Core · Blazor · SQLite · xUnit · Playwright browser flow · GitHub
      Actions — with the SDK pinned in <code>global.json</code> and restore run in locked mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={p.repo}>Repository</a> ·
      <a href={`${p.repo}/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Architecture</a> ·
      <a href={`${p.repo}/blob/v1.0.0/docs/READINESS_MODEL.md`}>Readiness model</a> ·
      <a href={`${p.repo}/blob/v1.0.0/docs/POLICY.md`}>Policy reference</a>
    </p>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .verdict-chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 6.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-geist-mono), ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.9rem;
    border-radius: 9999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .verdict-chip--go {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
    background: var(--accent-wash);
  }

  .verdict-chip--blocked {
    color: var(--caution);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--caution) 40%, transparent);
    background: var(--caution-wash);
  }
</style>
